  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Choanoeca flexa transcriptome and predicted nonredundant proteome (2019) in Figshare. 5 download(s) recorded.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Choanoeca flexa transcriptome and predicted nonredundant proteome (2019) in Figshare is authored by Brunet, Thibaut. Topics: Transcriptome, Proteome, Biology, Computational biology and Genetics.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">1 citations (OpenAlex). Citing venues include eLife.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "DataStory",
  "publisher": "Digital Science",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Figshare dataset has an early but concrete uptake record: one scholarly citation, two documented downstream reuse events, and modest usage with 16 views and 5 downloads. The record suggests the assembled Choanoeca flexa transcriptome and predicted proteome are already serving as a reference resource for comparative genomics and related studies.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2019 as an open dataset on Figshare, this resource provides an assembled transcriptome and predicted nonredundant proteins for the choanoflagellate Choanoeca flexa. Its concept profile is centered on transcriptome and proteome biology, with additional links to computational biology, genetics, proteomics, cell biology, and gene expression, matching the dataset\u2019s role as a foundational molecular reference rather than a conventional article.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact is still early and focused. OpenAlex records one citation, and Crossref data show two journal articles that reference the dataset as a source. The citing landscape is narrow but meaningful: the documented reuse comes from a 2025 eLife article on G-protein-coupled receptor diversity and evolution in close relatives of metazoans, indicating that the dataset is being used in comparative work on early-diverging lineages and receptor evolution.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data are modest, with 16 views and 5 downloads recorded, which is consistent with a specialized dataset that is primarily valuable to a focused research community. OpenAIRE also shows the dataset as openly accessible in multiple repository instances, supporting reuse without access barriers.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Public attention signals are limited in the available record: scite reports no supporting or contradicting mentions, and Event Data did not capture online mentions, while the public discourse archive is unavailable. Taken together, the evidence points to a small but credible pattern of scholarly reuse, with the clearest signal coming from downstream research applications rather than broader public visibility.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
}